# Batch run macro for cosmic muon simulation
# Usage: MuonSim [-o out.root] [-s seed] [-m materials.yml] run.mac
#        MuonSim --help   for the full option list

/run/initialize

/run/verbose 1
/event/verbose 0
/tracking/verbose 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RootManager options
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# /ROOT/savetrackopt <int>  — bit flags (OR together) for which tracks to save
#
#   0   kSaveNone     save nothing (default)
#   1   kSaveHeavy    muons, hadrons, ions   (and nothing else)
#   2   kSaveElec     e± NOT from EM-shower processes — decay electrons land here
#   4   kSaveGamma    gammas
#   8   kSaveEMShower e± from compt/phot/conv/annihil/Ioni/Brem
#  16   kSaveOptical  optical photons
#
#   The bits run in order of how often they are wanted, so the useful sets are the
#   low numbers: heavy, then decay electrons, then gammas, and only after those the
#   two that dominate the file size.
#
#   Examples:
#     /ROOT/savetrackopt 1    → muons/hadrons/ions only
#     /ROOT/savetrackopt 5    → those + gammas             (1+4)
#     /ROOT/savetrackopt 7    → those + gammas + decay e±  (1+2+4)
#     /ROOT/savetrackopt 15   → those + gammas + all e±    (1+2+4+8)
#     /ROOT/savetrackopt 31   → everything                 (1+2+4+8+16)
#
#   Gammas have a bit of their own because they are the background channel
#   underground work turns on: dark-matter and double-beta searches live or die by
#   them. They are also most of the output — 300 cosmic events at fixed seed 12345
#   give 2.39 MB at 1 and 9.57 MB at 5, so gammas are 75% of the file and 95% of
#   the tracks. Ask for them deliberately.
#
/ROOT/savetrackopt 1

# /ROOT/savestepopt <int>  — bit flags for which track types get step-level info saved
#
#   Same bit positions as savetrackopt.
#   NOTE: steps are stored inside the MCTrack object, so a step is saved only
#   when BOTH conditions hold:
#     (a) the track type bit is set in savetrackopt  (MCTrack exists)
#     (b) the track type bit is set in savestepopt
#   Effective step set = savetrackopt & savestepopt.
#
#   0   kStepNone     save no steps (default)
#   1   kStepHeavy    muons, hadrons, ions
#   2   kStepElec     e± NOT from EM-shower processes
#   4   kStepGamma    gammas
#   8   kStepEMShower e± from compt/phot/conv/annihil/Ioni/Brem
#  16   kStepOptical  optical photons  (WARNING: enormous output)
#
#   Examples:
#     /ROOT/savestepopt 1    → muon/hadron steps only  ← typical choice
#     /ROOT/savestepopt 5    → those + gamma steps       (needs savetrackopt bit 4)
#     /ROOT/savestepopt 7    → those + gamma + decay e±  (needs savetrackopt 7)
#
#   Each step carries the process that ENDED it (fProcessName) and, in a PSMD
#   slab, the slab it deposited in (fDetectorID). The process is what lets an
#   interaction be told from a boundary crossing: in a cosmic run only 1.9% of
#   saved steps are Transportation, the rest being compt/muIoni/Cerenkov/phot and
#   the rare nCapture, photonNuclear and muMinusCaptureAtRest.
#
/ROOT/savestepopt 1

# /ROOT/savehitphoton <int>  — per-PMT optical photon hit detail
#   0   off — save only PMT hit count per event (default)
#   1   on  — save individual photon hit info (time, position, ...) per PMT
#
/ROOT/savehitphoton 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optical properties of materials and surfaces
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Refractive indices, absorption and Rayleigh lengths, reflector reflectivity and
# photocathode efficiency all come from a YAML file, not from the code: they are
# the systematics of an optical detector, so varying one has to be possible without
# a rebuild.
#
# They are NOT set from this macro. What the detector IS -- its dimensions and its
# material properties -- is named on the command line, while a macro says what the
# run DOES. The reason is not tidiness: both have to be in place before
# /run/initialize builds the geometry, and a macro command that only works above a
# certain line is a trap. /material/load used to exist and was silently ignored
# when it came after /run/initialize.
#
#   MuonSim -m my_materials.yml run.mac
#
# With nothing given, data/optical/wcmd_materials.yml is used -- found next to the
# executable, or through $MUONSIM_DATA. A file need only carry what it changes;
# several may be given with repeated -m, each adding to the ones before. Schema and validation rules: src/phys/MaterialPropertyFile.hh
#
# The line the run prints names the file and a content digest, so an output can be
# traced back to the numbers that produced it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Primary generator — /gen/vertex
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# At least one /gen/vertex is REQUIRED -- there is no default generator, and
# /run/beamOn without one is a fatal error (GEN001). This macro asks for cosmic
# muons, which read the flux histogram h_flux from the named ROOT file; the
# alternatives below are commented out. Adding a second /gen/vertex does not
# replace the first, it fires alongside it.
#
# Each /gen/vertex adds one vertex and ALL of them fire in EVERY event. A vertex
# re-samples its position, energy and direction each event.
#
#   /gen/vertex <type> [<energy>] [clause] ...
#
# --- type ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   a Geant4 particle    e-, e+, mu-, gamma, alpha, neutron, proton, ...
#
#   an isotope           60Co, 232Th, 238U, 252Cf   (Co60 spelling also works)
#                        Leave the energy off so it starts at rest and decays;
#                        the daughter chain is followed. Restrict it with
#                          /process/had/rdm/nucleusLimits <aMin> <aMax> <zMin> <zMax>
#
#   a source             brings its own particles and energies, so it takes no
#                        energy spec:
#     cosmic             muons from a flux histogram. Derives its own position,
#                        so it takes no position clause. Launched from a sphere,
#                        set with the 'sphere' clause, and it sets the run's
#                        clock itself so a live time is reported.
#                        REQUIRES input <flux.root>: the SPHERE file, holding the
#                        TH3D h_flux. The PLANE file has cos(theta) folded in and
#                        would be wrong by 32%, silently.
#     rockgamma          gammas from the surrounding rock, entering through the
#                        cavern boundary.  REQUIRES input <spectrum.yml>
#     IBD                inverse beta decay -> correlated e+ and neutron.
#                        REQUIRES input <spectrum.yml>
#     AmBe               neutron + the 4.44 MeV 12C gamma (in 58% of emissions).
#                        Uses a built-in measured spectrum; an OPTIONAL
#                        input <spectrum.yml> replaces it
#     252CfSF            one spontaneous fission: ~3.8 neutrons, ~7 prompt
#                        gammas, every event. Takes no input file -- it has
#                        three built-in distributions, which one file cannot hold
#     file               ready-made events from a text file, for the output of a
#                        generator this code does not contain (DECAY0 and the
#                        like). The file gives the particles, the position clause
#                        says where, so one file serves any volume.
#                        REQUIRES input <events.txt>. Format: doc/generator.md
#
#   Spectra are YAML tables (schema in src/gen/SpectrumFile.hh) for every source
#   except cosmic: its flux is a 3D joint (energy, theta, phi) distribution, not a
#   table, so it stays a ROOT histogram.
#
#   AmBe is the only source with a built-in spectrum, because Am-Be has one
#   accepted model. IBD and rockgamma have no single standard spectrum -- reactor
#   vs geoneutrino, one rock composition vs another -- so they demand the file
#   rather than quietly inventing one.
#
#   NOTE 252Cf as a particle name is the Cf252 *ion*, decayed by Geant4: only ~3%
#   of decays fission. Use 252CfSF to get a fission every event.
#
# --- energy ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   Comes directly after the type. Omit it for 0, i.e. at rest. Unit defaults to
#   MeV (Geant4's native energy unit):
#
#     <value> [unit]                       fixed
#     uniform  <min> <max> [unit]          flat between the bounds
#     gauss    <mean> <sigma> [unit]       Gaussian, truncated at zero
#     exp      <E0> [unit]                 dN/dE ~ exp(-E/E0)
#     powerlaw <index> <min> <max> [unit]  dN/dE ~ E^index
#     spectrum <E>:<w> <E>:<w> ... [unit]  discrete lines, relative weights
#
# --- clauses (any order) ---------------------------------------------------
#
#   position — one of these is required (except for cosmic):
#     point <x> <y> <z> [unit]        fixed point; length unit defaults to mm
#     involume <volume>               uniform inside that physical volume
#     onvolume [in|out|both] <volume> uniform over its surface. in/out restricts
#                                     the emission to that side of the surface
#                                     normal; both (the default) is 4 pi
#     multivolume <pattern>           every volume whose name contains <pattern>,
#                                     picked in proportion to cubic volume
#
#   direction:
#     random                          isotropic (the default)
#     direction <dx> <dy> <dz>        fixed
#     cos | iso                       how a restricted surface spreads its
#                                     emission. iso is uniform in solid angle,
#                                     right for activity decaying on a surface.
#                                     cos follows Lambert, right for a flux
#                                     crossing it, and is rockgamma's default.
#
#   other:
#     polarization <px> <py> <pz>     a single named particle only; a source sets
#                                     its own, and the clause is refused there
#     time <t> [unit]                 vertex time, default ns
#     input <file>                    data file for the sources above:
#                                     .yml/.yaml table, .root histogram, or the
#                                     event list 'file' reads
#     sphere <x> <y> <z> <R> [unit]   cosmic only: the surface muons are launched
#                                     from. R only has to enclose what is being
#                                     asked about; the answer does not depend on
#                                     it and the cost goes as R^2
#     rotate                          file only: turn each event to a random
#                                     orientation as it is used
#
#   The world is a SPHERE of radius 16 m centred on the origin, mostly rock. The
#   air cavern is the upper half of a 15 m sphere standing on the cavern floor at
#   z = 0, which is the datum every structure is measured from. Useful coordinates
#   are a few metres up, not around the origin:
#
#     hall floor        0          shielding top   3865
#     pit bottom       -5000       frame top       4550  = tank floor
#     water         4584..7450     tank top        7692
#     hall apex     15000          rock shell       1000
#                                  world radius    16500
#
#   A vertex outside the world aborts the run reporting the offending coordinates
#   instead of crashing inside the navigator.
#
#   Physical volume names in this geometry:
#     hall:  World  Rock  Hall      (Hall is the air the detectors stand in -- the
#                                    dome AND the pit, one volume at the origin, so
#                                    hall coordinates ARE world coordinates)
#     WCMD:  TankSteel  DoorSteel  TankAir  TankWater  DoorWater
#            RoomSteel  RoomAir  PMTPhys0 .. PMTPhys47
#     PSMD:  PSMDModule0 .. PSMDModule129   one module each
#            PSMDAlShell  PSMDScintTop  PSMDScintBottom
#     frame: HBeam0 .. HBeam15   columns and top girders, exact H sections
#     shield: ShieldHDPE  ShieldBoratedRubber  ShieldLead  ShieldLeadInner
#            ShieldCavity   (the air the shielding encloses, open at the top)
#   (use "multivolume PMTPhys" for all 48 PMTs, "multivolume PSMDModule" for all
#    130 modules)
#
#   NOTE the PSMD module is ONE logical volume placed 130 times, so the three
#   names inside it are shared by all of them: "involume PSMDScintTop" resolves to
#   whichever module the volume search reaches first, not to all 130. To aim at a
#   particular module use its own name, e.g. "involume PSMDModule65".
#
#   Which PSMD slab a step deposited energy in is recorded as MCStep::fDetectorID:
#     detectorID = 2 * module + slab,   slab 0 = top, 1 = bottom,  -1 = not in a slab
#   so module = detectorID / 2. The id names a slab; readout channels (two per
#   slab, four per module) are a level below and are not simulated yet.
#
# --- examples --------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   2.5 MeV electron straight up from a fixed point
#     /gen/vertex e- 2.5 MeV point 0 0 5000 mm direction 0 0 1
#
#   flat 0-3 MeV electron anywhere in the water, isotropic
#     /gen/vertex e- uniform 0 3 MeV involume TankWater random
#
#   60Co decaying in the water: bulk contamination
#     /gen/vertex 60Co involume TankWater
#
#   40K gamma from the 48 PMTs, weighted by their volume
#     /gen/vertex gamma 1.46 MeV multivolume PMTPhys
#
#   2.615 MeV line smeared by 50 keV
#     /gen/vertex gamma gauss 2.615 0.05 MeV involume TankWater
#
#   alpha lines with relative intensities
#     /gen/vertex alpha spectrum 5.407:1 5.685:0.2 MeV involume TankWater
#
#   surface contamination on the steel, emitted into the tank only
#     /gen/vertex neutron 1 MeV onvolume in TankSteel
#
#   cosmic-like muon spectrum, straight down (note the explicit GeV)
#     /gen/vertex mu- powerlaw -2.7 1 100 GeV point 0 0 9000 mm direction 0 0 -1
#
#   calibration sources
#     /gen/vertex AmBe point 0 0 5000 mm
#     /gen/vertex AmBe involume TankWater input data/ambe_spectrum_example.yml
#     /gen/vertex 252CfSF involume TankWater
#     /gen/vertex IBD involume TankWater input data/ibd_spectrum_example.yml
#
#   gammas from the rock, entering through the hall wall. Use Hall, not
#   World: the world's own skin is now the OUTSIDE of the rock, so starting there
#   buries the gammas under a metre of it
#     /gen/vertex rockgamma onvolume in Hall input data/rock_gamma_example.yml
#
#   the cosmic muon generator, stated explicitly
#     /gen/vertex cosmic input data/muon_flux.root
#
#   two vertices, both firing every event
#     /gen/vertex e- 1 MeV point -1000 0 5000 mm
#     /gen/vertex gamma 1 MeV point 1000 0 5000 mm
#
# --- the other /gen commands ----------------------------------------------
#
#   /gen/list     print the vertices defined so far
#   /gen/clear    remove them all; one must be defined again before /run/beamOn
#
#   /control/manual /gen/vertex    full syntax reference at run time
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/gen/vertex cosmic input data/muon_flux.root

/run/beamOn {nevent}
